  "thumbnailAlt": "Maintenance Request form beside a photo of a man fixing a ceiling light with a toolbox nearby",
  "content": {
    "intro": "A maintenance request form is a form that lets tenants report a repair with their unit number, the problem area, how urgent it is, photos and permission to enter so a landlord or property manager can send the right person quickly. This template separates emergencies like leaks and no heat from routine fixes and records entry permission in writing.",
    "audience": "Landlords, property management companies, HOAs, student housing and apartment communities, and building superintendents.",
    "useCases": [
      "A repair link in the tenant welcome pack and lease email",
      "A QR code in the lobby, laundry room or each unit",
      "Student housing teams handling move-in repair lists",
      "HOAs collecting common-area maintenance reports"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Print a QR code on a sticker inside each unit's kitchen cabinet so tenants never have to hunt for the link.",
      "Turn on the respondent email copy so tenants have a dated record of every request they submit.",
      "Edit responses to log the vendor, visit date and completion; the edit history gives you a repair trail for each unit."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should a maintenance request form include?",
        "answer": "The tenant's name and contact details, the property address and unit number, the area and type of problem, how urgent it is, a description, photos, whether the tenant gives permission to enter when they are out, pets on site and the best times for access."
      },
      {
        "question": "What counts as an emergency maintenance request?",
        "answer": "Problems that put people or the property at immediate risk: a gas smell, flooding or a burst pipe, no heat in freezing weather, a total power loss, a broken exterior lock or sewage backup. Tenants should also call the emergency line for these rather than only filling in a form."
      },
      {
        "question": "How long does a landlord have to make repairs?",
        "answer": "It depends on where the property is and how serious the problem is. Many places require urgent repairs within a day or a few days and other repairs within a reasonable time, often around 30 days. Check your local landlord-tenant rules."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does a landlord need permission to enter for repairs?",
        "answer": "Most places require reasonable notice before entering, except in emergencies, and the required notice period varies. Asking tenants for written permission to enter on the form makes the visit easier to arrange; check your local rules and lease terms."
      }
    ]
  },
